\id 2CO Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 2 Corinthians
\toc1 The Second Epistle of Paul to the Corinthians.
\toc2 2 Corinthians
\toc3 2CO
\mt1 The Second Epistle of Paul to the Corinthians.
\is THE ARGUMENT OF THE SECOND EPISTLE TO THE CORINTHIANS.
\ip FOR the time when this Epiſtle was written, looke the Argument of the epiſtle to the Romanes: to wit, about the eightenth yere after his conuerſion, & our Lordes paſſion, becauſe in the 11. chapter he maketh mention of 14 yeres, not only after his Conuerſion, as to the Galatians , but \f - \ft \+it 2. Cor. 11. 2.\+it*\f* \f - \ft \+it Gal. 2. 1.\+it*\f* alſo after his rapte, which ſeemeth to haue bene when he was at Hieruſalem Act. 9, 26. foure yeres after his Conuerſion (Gal. 1, 18) in a traunce or exceſſe of minde, as he calleth it, Act. 22, 17. It was written at Troas (it is thought) and ſent by Titus, as we reade chap. 8.
\ip It is for the moſt part againſt thoſe falſe Apoſtles whom in the firſt part of the firſt to the Corinthians, he noted, or rather ſpared, but now is conſtrained to deale openly againſt them, & to defend both his owne perſon which they ſought to bring into contempt, making way thereby to the correption of the Corinthians, and withall to mainteine the excellencie of the Miniſterie and Miniſters of the new Teſtament, aboue which they did magnifie the Miniſterie of the old Teſtament: bearing themſelves very high becauſe they were Jews.
\ip Againſt theſe therefore S. Paule auoucheth the preeminent power of his Miniſterie, by which power alſo he giueth a pardon to the inceſtuous fornicator whom he excommunicated in the laſt Epiſtle, ſeeing now his penance, and againe threateneth to come & excõmunicate thoſe that had greuouſly ſinned and remained impenitent. Two chapters alſo he interpoſeth of the contributions to the Church of Hieruſalem, mentioned in his laſt, exhorting them to doe liberally, and alſo to haue all in a readines againſt his comming.
\c 1
\cd By his troubles in Aſia he comforteth them. and (againſt his Aduerſaries the falſe apoſtles of the Iewes) alleageth to them the teſtimonie of his ovvne and alſo of their conſcience, 17 anſwering them that obiected lightnes againſt him, for not comming to Corinth according to his promis.
\p
\v 1 \sc PAVL\sc* an Apoſtle of \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt by the vvil of God, and Timothee our brother: to the Church of God that is at Corinth, vvith al the ſaincts that are in al Achaia.
\v 2 Grace vnto you and peace from God our father, and from our Lord \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt.
\v 3 \f 1 \fr 1.3 \ft The Epiſtle for a Martyr that is a Biſhop.\f* Bleſſed be the God and father of our Lord \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt, the father of mercies, and God of al comfort,
\v 4 vvho comforteth vs in al our tribulation: that vve alſo may be able to comfort them that are in all diſtreſſe, \f 1 \fr 1.4 \ft \+it c διὰ τῆς παρακλήσεως ἕις παρακαλούμεθα.\+it*\f* by the exhortation vvherevvith vve alſo are exhorted of God.
\v 5 For as the paſſions of Chriſt abound in vs: ſo alſo by Chriſt doth our comfort abound.
\v 6 And vvhether vve be in tribulation, for your exhortation and ſaluation: vvhether vve be exhorted, for your exhortation and ſaluation, vvhich vvorketh the toleration of the ſame paſſions vvhich vve alſo doe ſuffer:
\v 7 and our hope is firme for you: knovving that as you are partakers of the paſſions, ſo ſhal you be of the conſolation alſo. ⊢
\v 8 For we wil not haue you ignorant brethren: concerning our tribulation, vvhich happened in Aſia, that vve vvere preſſed aboue meaſure aboue our povver, ſo that it vvas tedious vnto vs euen to liue.
\v 9 But vve in our ſelues had the anſvver of death, that vve be not truſting in our ſelues, but in God vvho raiſeth vp the dead,
\v 10 vvho hath deliuered and doth deliuer vs out of ſo great dangers: in vvhom vve hope that he vvil yet alſo deliuer vs,
\v 11 you helping vvithal in praier for vs, that by many mens perſons, thankes for that gift vvhich is in vs, may be giuen by many in our behalfe.
\v 12 For our glorie is this, the teſtimonie of our conſcience, that in ſimplicity and ſincerity of God, and not in carnal wiſedom, but in the grace of God vve haue conuerſed in this vvorld: and more aboundantly tovvards you.
\v 13 For vve vvrite no other things to you, then that you haue read and knovv. And I hope that you ſhal knovv vnto the ende:
\v 14 as alſo you haue knovven vs in part, that vve are your glorie, as you alſo ours in the day of our Lord \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt.
\v 15 And in this confidence I vvould firſt haue come to you, that you might haue a ſecond grace:
\v 16 and by you paſſe into Macedonia, and againe from Macedonia come to you, and of you be brought on my vvay into Iewrie.
\v 17 VVhereas then I vvas thus minded, did I vſe lightenes? Or the things that I minde, do I minde according to the fleſh, that there be vvith me, \it It is\it* and \it It is not\it*?
\v 18 But God is faithful, becauſe our preaching vvhich vvas to you, there is not in it, \it It is\it*, and, \it It is not\it*.
\v 19 For the Sonne of God \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt, vvho by vs vvas preached among you, by me and Syluanus and Timothee, vvas not, \it It is\it*, and, \it It is not\it*. but, \it It is\it*, vvas in him.
\v 20 For al the promiſes of God that are, in him \it It is\it*: therefore alſo by him, Amen to God, vnto our glorie.
\v 21 And he that confirmeth vs vvith you in Chriſt, and that hath anointed vs, God:
\v 22 vvho alſo hath ſealed vs, and giuen the pledge of the Spirit in our hartes.
\v 23 And I call God to vvitneſſe vpon my ſoul, that ſparing you, I came not any more to Corinth,
\v 24 not becauſe vve ouerrule your faith: but, we are helpers of your ioy. for in the faith you ſtand.
\c 2
\cd Proſecuting the true cauſe which in the laſt chapter he gaue of his not comming, 6 he pardoneth novv after ſome part of penance, him that for inceſt he excommunicated in the laſt epiſtle, requiring them obediently to conſent therevnto. 12 Then, of his going from Troas into Macedonia, God euery where giuing him the triumph.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* I haue determined vvith my ſelf this ſame thing, not to come to you againe in ſorovv.
\v 2 For if I make you ſorie: and who is it that can make me glad, but he that is made ſorie by me?
\v 3 And this ſame I vvrote to you: that I may not, vvhen I come, haue ſorovv vpon ſorovv, of the vvhich I ought to reioyce: truſting in you al, that my ioy is the ioy of you al.
\v 4 For of much tribulation and anguiſh of hart I vvrote to you by many teares: not that you ſhould be made ſorie: but that you may knovv vvhat charitie I haue more aboundantly tovvard you.
\v 5 And if any man hath made ſorovvful, not me hath he made ſorovvful, but in part, that I burden not al you.
\v 6 To him that is ſuch a one, this rebuke ſufficeth that is giuen of many:
\v 7 ſo that contrariewiſe you ſhould rather pardon and comfort him, leſt perhaps ſuch an one be ſvvallovved vp vvith ouer great ſorow.
\v 8 For the vvhich cauſe I beſeeche you that you confirme charitie tovvard him.
\v 9 For therfore alſo haue I written that I may knovv the experiment of you, vvhether in al things you be obedient.
\v 10 And whom you haue pardoned any thing, I alſo. For, my ſelf alſo that which \f 1 \fr 2.10 \ft \+it c κεχάρισμαι.\+it* Though he did great Penance (ſaith Theodorete) yet he calleth this pardoning. \+it χάριν, a grace,\+it* becauſe his ſinne was\f* I pardoned, if I pardoned any thing, for you in the perſon of Chriſt,
\v 11 that vve be not circumuented of Satan. for vve are not ignorant of his cogitations.
\v 12 And vvhen I vvas come to Troas for the Goſpel of Chriſt, and a doore vvas opened vnto me in our Lord,
\v 13 I had no reſt in my ſpirit, for that I found not Titus my brother, but bidding them fare vvel, I vvent forth into Macedonia.
\v 14 And thankes be to God, vvho alvvaies triumpheth vs in Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc*, and manifeſteth the odour of his knowledge by vs in euery place.
\v 15 For we are the good odour of Chriſt vnto God in them that are ſaued, and in them that periſh.
\v 16 To ſome in deede the odour of death vnto death: but to others the odour of life vnto life. And to theſe things vvho is ſo ſufficient?
\v 17 For we are not as very many, adulterating the vvord of God, but of ſinceritie, and as of God, before God, in Chriſt vve ſpeake.
\c 3
\cd Leſt the Iudaical falſe Apoſtles ſhould obiect againe that he praiſeth him ſelf, he ſaith that the Corinthians are his commendation: and they in their hartes being iuſtified by his miniſterie, he thereof inferreth that the miniſters of the new Teſtament are farre more glorious then they of the old, 12 and our people more lightened then theirs.
\p
\v 1 \sc BEGIN\sc* we againe to commend our ſelues? or do we neede (as certaine) epiſtles of commendation to you, or from you?
\v 2 Our epiſtle you are, vvritten in our hartes, vvhich is knovven and read of al men:
\v 3 being manifeſted that you are the epiſtle of Chriſt, miniſtred by vs, & vvritten not vvith inke, but with the Spirit of the liuing God: not in tables of ſtone, but in the tables carnall of the hart.
\v 4 \f 1 \fr 3.4 \ft The Epiſtle vpon the 12 Sunday after Pentecoſt.\f* And ſuch confidence vve haue by Chriſt to God:
\v 5 not that vve be ſufficient to thinke any thing of our ſelues, as of our ſelues: but our ſufficiencie is of God.
\v 6 VVho alſo hath made vs meete miniſters of the nevv teſtament: not in the letter, but in the Spirit. For the letter killeth: but the Spirit quickeneth.
\v 7 And if the miniſtration of death with letters figured in ſtones, vvas in glorie, ſo that the children of Iſraël could not \f 1 \fr 3.7 \ft The Apoſtles vvrote the Goſpel in men's hearts much more than in paper.\f* behold the face of Moyſes, for the glorie of his countenance, that is made voide:
\v 8 how ſhal not the miniſtration of the Spirit be more in glorie?
\v 9 For if the miniſtration of damnation be in glorie: much more the miniſterie of iuſtice aboundeth in glorie. ⊢
\v 10 For neither vvas it glorified, vvhich in this part vvas glorious, by reaſon of the excelling glorie.
\v 11 For if that which is made void, is by glorie: much more that vvhich abideth, is in glorie.
\v 12 Hauing therfore ſuch hope, vve vſe much confidence:
\v 13 and not \x - \xt Exo. 34, 33.\x* as Moyſes put a vele vpon his face, that the children of Iſraël might not behold his face, vvhich is made voide,
\v 14 but their ſenſes vvere dulled. For vntil this preſent day, the ſelf ſame vele in the lecture of the old teſtament remaineth vnreuealed (becauſe in Chriſt it is made voide)
\v 15 but vntil this preſent day, vvhen Moyſes is read, a vele is put vpon their hart.
\v 16 But vvhen he ſhal be conuerted to our Lord, the vele ſhal be taken avvay.
\v 17 And \x - \xt Io. 4, 24.\x* our Lord is a Spirit. And vvhere the Spirit of our Lord is, there is libertie.
\v 18 But vve al, beholding the glorie of our Lord vvith face reuealed, are transformed into the ſame image from glorie vnto glorie, as of our Lordes Spirit.
\c 4
\cd That according as ſo glorious a miniſterie requireth, he liueth and preacheth ſincerely. 7 the which glorie his Aduerſaries can not count vaine, conſidering his perſecutions, because perſecution is to Gods glorie, and to our humilitie and hope, and meritorious of increaſe of grace in this life, and of moſt glorious bodies and ſoules afterward.
\p
\v 1 \sc THERFORE\sc* hauing this miniſtration: according as vve haue obteined mercie, we faile not,
\v 2 but vve renounce the ſecrete things of diſhoneſtie, not walking in craftines, nor adulterating the vvord of God, but in manifeſtation of the truth commending our ſelues to euery conſcience of men before God.
\v 3 And if our Goſpel be alſo hidde, in them that periſh it is hidde,
\v 4 in whom the God of this vvorld hath blinded the mindes of the infidels, that the illumination of the Goſpel of the glorie of Chriſt vvho is the image of God, might not ſhine to them.
\v 5 \f 1 \fr 4.5 \ft The Epiſtle for S. Athanaſius. \+it Mai. 2.\+it*\f* For vve preache not our ſelues, but \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt our Lord: and vs, your ſeruants by \sc IESVS\sc*,
\v 6 becauſe God that commaunded light to ſhine of darkenes, he hath ſhined in our hartes to the illumination of the knovvledge of the glorie of God, in the face of Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc*.
\v 7 But we haue this treaſure in earthen veſſels, that the excellencie may be of the povver of God, and not of vs.
\v 8 In al things vve ſuffer tribulation, but are not in diſtreſſe: \f 1 \fr 4.8 \ft I \+it c aporiamur. See S. Ambr. Theoph.\+it*\f* vve vvant, but are not deſtitute:
\v 9 vve ſuffer perſecution, but are not forſaken: vve are caſt dovvne, but we periſh not:
\v 10 alvvaies bearing about in our body the mortification of \sc IESVS\sc*, that the life alſo of \sc IESVS\sc* may be manifeſted in our bodies.
\v 11 For vve that liue, are alvvaies deliuered vnto death for \sc IESVS\sc*: that the life alſo of \sc IESVS\sc* may be manifeſted in our mortal fleſh.
\v 12 Death then vvorketh in vs, but life in you.
\v 13 And hauing the ſame ſpirit of faith, as it is vvritten, \x - \xt Ps. 115, 10.\x* \it I beleeued, for the vvhich cauſe I haue ſpoken\it*, vve alſo beleeue, for the vvhich cauſe vve ſpeake alſo:
\v 14 knovving that he vvhich raiſed vp \sc IESVS\sc*, vvil raiſe vp vs alſo vvith \sc IESVS\sc*, and ſet vs vvith you. ⊢
\v 15 For al things are for you: that the grace abounding by many in giuing of thankes, may abound vnto the glorie of God.
\v 16 For vvhich cauſe vve faile not: but although that our man vvhich is vvithout, \f 1 \fr 4.16 \ft \+it b corrumpitur.\+it*\f* bcorrupte: yet that vvhich is vvithin, is renevved from day to day.
\v 17 For that our tribulation vvhich preſently is momentanie & light, \f 1 \fr 4.17 \ft The Engliſh Bible 1577, doth falſely tranſlate, \+it prepareth\+it*.\f*\f 2 \fr 4.17 \ft \+it c κατεργάζεται.\+it*\f* vvorketh aboue meaſure excedingly an eternal vveight of glorie in vs,
\v 18 we not conſidering the things that are ſeen, but that are not ſeen. For the things that be ſeen, are temporal: but thoſe that be not ſeen, are eternal.
\c 5
\cd That after death of the body the ſoule may to heauen: therfore, although naturally we abhorre death, by grace he deſireth it rather: 9 in conſideration of Chriſtes iuſt iudgement, liuing as in the ſight of God, yea and of their conſciences. 12 Which he ſpeaketh not to praiſe him ſelf, but becauſe of his Aduerſaries who did glorie in carnal reſpectes: but he and the other Apoſtles regard nothing but their reconciliation vnto God by Chriſt, and to reconcile others alſo, as being his legates for that purpoſe.
\p
\v 1 \sc FOR\sc* vve knovv that if our earthly houſe of this habitation be diſſolued, that vve haue a building of God, a houſe not made vvith hand, eternal in heauen.
\v 2 For in this alſo do vve grone, deſirous to be ouerclothed with our habitation that is from heauen:
\v 3 yet ſo, if we be found clothed, not naked.
\v 4 For vve alſo that are in this tabernacle, grone being burdened: becauſe vve would not be ſpoiled, but ouerclothed, that that vvhich is mortal, might be ſwallowed vp of life.
\v 5 And he that maketh vs to this ſame, is God, vvho hath giuen vs the pledge of the Spirit.
\v 6 Being bold therfore alvvaies, and knovving that vvhile vve are in the body, we are pilgrimes from God,
\v 7 (for vve vvalke by faith and not by ſight)
\v 8 but we are bold, and haue a good vvil to be pilgrimes rather from the body, & \f 1 \fr 5.8 \ft This place proueth that the Saincts departed now ſince Chriſt, ſleepe not til the day of iudgement, and that they be not holden in any ſeueral place of reſt from the fruition of God til the reſurrection of their bodies, but that they be preſent with God in their ſoules.\f* to be preſent vvith our Lord.
\v 9 And therfore vve endeuour, vvhether abſent or preſent, to pleaſe him.
\v 10 For \x - \xt Ro. 14, 10.\x* vve muſt al be manifeſted before the iudgement ſeate of Chriſt, that euery one may receiue the proper things of the body, according as he hath done, either good or euil.
\v 11 Knovving therfore the feare of our Lord vve vſe perſuaſion to men: but to God we are manifeſt. And I hope alſo that in your conſciences vve are manifeſt.
\v 12 We commend not our ſelues againe to you, but giue you occaſion to glorie for vs: that you may haue againſt them that glorie in face, and not in hart.
\v 13 for vvhether vve excede in minde, to God: or vvhether vve be ſober, to you.
\v 14 For the charitie of Chriſt vrgeth vs: iudging this, that if one died for al, then al vvere dead.
\v 15 and Chriſt died for al: that they alſo vvhich liue, may not now liue to them ſelues, but to him that died for them and roſe againe.
\v 16 Therfore vve from hence forth knovv no man according to the fleſh. And if vve haue knovven Chriſt according to the fleſh: but now we know him no more.
\v 17 If then any be in Chriſt a nevv creature: the old are paſſed, behold \x - \xt Eſa. 43. 19. Apoc. 21, 5.\x* al things are made nevv.
\v 18 but al of God, vvho hath reconciled vs to him ſelf by Chriſt: and hath giuen vs the miniſterie of reconciliation.
\v 19 For God in deede vvas in Chriſt reconciling the vvorld to him ſelf, not imputing to them their ſinnes, and hath put in vs the vvord of reconciliation.
\v 20 For Chriſt therfore vve are legates, God as it vvere exhorting by vs. For Chriſt vve beſeeche you, be reconciled to God.
\v 21 Him that knevv no ſinne, for vs he made \f 1 \fr 5.21 \ft That is to ſay, a ſacrifice and an hoſt for ſinne. \+it See the laſt annot. of this chapter\+it*.\f* ſinne: that vve might be made the iuſtice of God in him.
\c 6
\cd That he helpeth vvith his exhortations, and in al things behaueth him ſelf as becommeth a miniſter of God. 11 Which he ſpeaketh ſo openly, becauſe his hart is open vnto them: exhorting them to be likevviſe open-harted tovvardes him, 14 and to auoid thoſe Infidels.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* \f 1 \fr 6.1 \ft The Epiſtle vpon the firſt Sunday of Lent.\f* vve helping do exhorte, that you receiue not the grace of God in vaine.
\v 2 ( For he ſaith, \x - \xt Eſa. 49, 8.\x* \it In time accepted haue I heard thee: and in the day of ſaluation haue I holpen thee\it*. Behold, novv is the time acceptable: behold novv the day of ſaluation.)
\v 3 to no man giuing any offence, that our miniſterie be not blamed:
\v 4 \f 1 \fr 6.4 \ft The Epiſtle for many Martyrs.\f* but in al things let vs exhibite our ſelues as the miniſters of God, in much patience, in tribulations, in neceſſities, in diſtreſſes,
\v 5 in ſtripes, in priſons, in ſeditions, in labours, in vvatchings, in faſtings,
\v 6 in chaſtitie, in knovvledge, in longanimitie, in ſweetenes, in the holy Ghoſt, in charitie not feined,
\v 7 in the vvord of truth, in the vertue of God, by the armour of iuſtice on the right hand, and on the left,
\v 8 by honour and diſhonour, by infamie and good fame: as ſeducers, and true: as they that are vnknovven and knovven:
\v 9 as dying, and behold we liue: as chaſtened, & not killed:
\v 10 as ſorovvful, but alvvaies reioycing: as needie, but enriching many: as \f 1 \fr 6.10 \ft S. Auguſtine (in ps. 113) gathereth hereby, that the Apoſtles did vow pouertie.\f* hauing nothing, and poſſeſſing al things. ⊢
\v 11 Our mouth is open to you ô Corinthians, our hart is dilated.
\v 12 You are not ſtraitened in vs: but in your ovvne bovvels you are ſtraitened.
\v 13 But hauing the ſame revvard (I ſpeake as to my children) be you alſo dilated.
\v 14 \f 1 \fr 6.14 \ft It is not lawful for Catholikes to marie vvith Heretikes or Infidels. See S. Hierom. cont. Iouinian. li. 1. Conc. Laod. c. 10 & 31.\f* Beare not the yoke vvith infidels. For vvhat participation hath iuſtice vvith iniquitie? or vvhat ſocietie is there betwene light and darkenes?
\v 15 And vvhat agreement vvith Chriſt and Belial? or vvhat part hath the faithful vvith the infidel?
\v 16 And vvhat agreement hath the temple of God vvith Idols? For you are the temple of the liuing God. as God ſaith, \x - \xt Lev. 26, 11.\x* \it That I wil dvvel, and walke in them, and vvil be their God: and they ſhal be my people\it*.
\v 17 For the vvhich cauſe, \x - \xt Eſa. 52, 11.\x* \it Goe out of the middes of them, and ſeparate your ſelues\it*, ſaith our Lord, \it and touch not the vncleane: and I wil receiue you\it*.
\v 18 \x - \xt Hier. 31, 1.\x* \it and I vvil be a father to you: and you ſhal be my ſonnes and daughters, ſaith our Lord omnipotent\it*.
\c 7
\cd He proceedeth to exhorte them to puritie, and to receiue him into their charitie. 3 Which leſt they ſhould thinke he ſpeaketh to accuſe them, he commendeth them highly, both for their behauiour tovvard Titus, and for their penance vvhich they had done vpon his other epiſtle.
\p
\v 1 \sc HAVING\sc* therfore theſe promiſſes, my deereſt, let vs cleanſe our ſelues from al inquination of the fleſh and ſpirit, perfiting ſanctification in the feare of God.
\v 2 Receiue vs. VVe haue hurt no man, vve haue corrupted no man, vve haue circumuented no man.
\v 3 I ſpeake not to your condemnation. for I ſaid before that you are in our hartes to die together and to liue together.
\v 4 Much is my confidence vvith you, much is my glorying for you: I am repleniſhed vvith conſolation: I do excedingly abound in ioy in al our tribulation.
\v 5 For alſo vvhen we were come into Macedonia, our fleſh had no reſt, but vve ſuffered al tribulation: without, combats: vvithin, feares.
\v 6 But God that comforteth the humble, did comforte vs, in the comming of Titus.
\v 7 And not only in his comming, but alſo in the conſolation, vvhervvith he vvas comforted among you, reporting to vs your deſire, your vveeping, your emulation for me, ſo that I reioyced the more.
\v 8 For although I made you ſorie in an epiſtle, it repenteth me not: albeit it repented me, ſeing that the ſame epiſtle (although but for a time) did make you ſorie.
\v 9 Now I am glad: not becauſe you vvere made ſorie, but becauſe you vvere made ſorie to penance. For you vvere made ſorie according to God, that in nothing you ſhould ſuffer detriment by vs.
\v 10 For \f 1 \fr 7.10 \ft Contrition or ſorovvful lamenting of our offenſes, is the cauſe of ſaluation. Not onely faith then ſaueth, as the Heretikes affirme.\f* the ſorovv that is according to God, vvorketh penance vnto ſaluation that is ſtable: but the ſorovv of the vvorld vvorketh death.
\v 11 For behold this very thing, that you vvere made ſorie according to God, hovv great carefulnes it vvorketh in you: yea defenſe, yea indignation, yea feare, yea deſire, yea emulation, yea reuenge. in al things you haue ſhewed your ſelues to be vndefiled in the matter.
\v 12 Therfore although I vvrote to you, not for him that did the iniurie, nor for him that ſuffered: but to manifeſt our carefulnes that vve haue for you before God,
\v 13 therfore vve are comforted. But in our conſolation, vve did the more aboundantly reioyce vpon the ioy of Titus, becauſe his ſpirit vvas refreſhed of al you.
\v 14 And if to him I gloried any thing of you, I am not confounded: but as vve ſpake al things to you in truth, ſo alſo our glorying that vvas to Titus, is made a truth,
\v 15 and his bovvels are more aboundantly toward you: remembring the obedience of you al, hovv vvith feare and trembling you receiued him.
\v 16 I reioyce that in al things I haue confidence in you.
\c 8
\cd By the example of the poore Macedonians he exhorteth them to contribute largely vnto the Church of Hieruſalem, 7 and by praiſing of them, 9 and by the example of Chriſt. 14 and by their owne ſpiritual profite in being partakers of that Churches merites, 16 and by commending the collectors that he ſendeth.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* vve doe you to vnderſtand, brethren, the grace of God, that is giuen in the churches of Macedonia,
\v 2 that in much experience of tribulation they had aboundance of ioy, & their very deepe pouertie abounded vnto the riches of their ſimplicitie,
\v 3 for according to their povver (I giue them teſtimonie) and aboue their povver they vvere willing,
\v 4 vvith much exhortation requeſting vs the grace and communication of the miniſterie that is done tovvard the ſaincts.
\v 5 And not as vve hoped, but their ovvne ſelues they gaue, firſt to our Lord, \f 1 \fr 8.5 \ft The principal reſpecte next after God, is to be had of our maiſters in religion, in al temporal and ſpiritual dueties.\f* then to vs by the vvil of God:
\v 6 in ſo much that vve deſired Titus, that as he began, ſo alſo he vvould perfit in you this grace alſo.
\v 7 But as in al things you abound in faith, and vvord, and knovvledge, & al carefulnes, moreouer alſo in your charitie tovvard vs, that in this grace alſo you may abounde.
\v 8 I ſpeake not as commaunding: but by the carefulnes of others, approuing alſo the good diſpoſition of your charitie.
\v 9 \f 1 \fr 8.9 \ft The Epiſtle for S. Paulinus, \+it Iun. 22.\+it*\f* For you knovv the grace of our Lord \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt, that for you he vvas made poore, whereas he vvas riche: that by his pouertie you might be riche.
\v 10 And in this point I giue counſel: for this is profitable for you, vvhich haue begonne not only to doe, but alſo to be willing, from the yere paſt:
\v 11 but novv perfourme ye it alſo in deede: that as your minde is prompt to be vvilling, ſo it may be alſo to perfourme, of that vvhich you haue.
\v 12 For if the vvil be prompt: it is accepted according to that vvhich it hath, not according to that which it hath not.
\v 13 For not that other ſhould haue eaſe, and you tribulation: but by an equalitie.
\v 14 Let in this preſent time your aboundance ſupplie their want: that their aboundance alſo may ſupplie your vvant, that there be an equalitie,
\v 15 as it is vvritten: \x - \xt Exo. 16, 28.\x* \it He that had much, abounded not: and he that had litle, vvanted not\it*. ⊢
\v 16 \f 1 \fr 8.16 \ft The Epiſtle vpon S. Lukes day, \+it Octob. 18\+it*.\f* And thankes be to God, that hath giuen the ſelf ſame carefulnes for you in the hart of Titus,
\v 17 for that he admitted in deede exhortation: but being more careful, of his ovvne vvil he vvent vnto you.
\v 18 VVe haue ſent alſo vvith him the brother, vvhoſe praiſe is in the Goſpel through al the churches:
\v 19 & not only that, but alſo he vvas ordeined of the churches fellovv of our peregrination, for this grace vvhich is miniſtred of vs to the glorie of our Lord, and our determined vvil:
\v 20 auoiding this, leſt any man might reprehend vs in this fulnes that is miniſtred of vs.
\v 21 For vve prouide good things \x - \xt Ro. 12, 17.\x* not only before God, but alſo before men.
\v 22 And vve haue ſent with them our brother alſo, vvhom vve haue proued in many things often to be careful: but now much more careful, for the great confidence in you,
\v 23 either for Titus vvhich is my fellovv and coadiutor tovvard you, or our brethren Apoſtles of the churches, the glorie of Chriſt.
\v 24 The declaration therfore vvhich is of your charitie and our glorying for you, declare ye tovvard them in the face of the churches. ⊢
\c 9
\cd He proceedeth exhorting them to the foreſaid contribution, 3 to verifie his commending of them. 6 and to do it liberally, that ſo they may merite the more, and God be the more praiſed.
\p
\v 1 \sc FOR\sc* concerning the miniſterie that is done tovvard the ſainctes, it is ſuperfluous for me to vvrite vnto you.
\v 2 For I know your prompt minde: for the which I glorie of you to the Macedonians: That Achaia alſo is ready from the yere paſt, and your emulation hath prouoked very many.
\v 3 But I haue ſent the brethren, that the thing vvhich vve glorie of you, be not made voide in this behalfe, that (as I haue ſaid) you may be ready:
\v 4 leſt vvhen the Macedonians ſhal come vvith me, and finde you vnready, vve (that vve ſay not, ye) may be aſhamed \f 1 \fr 9.4 \ft c That is, in this matter of alms Chryſos. Theoph.\f* in this ſubſtance.
\v 5 Therfore I thought it neceſſarie to deſire the brethren that they vvould come to you, and prepare this bleſſing before promiſed, to be ready ſo, as a bleſſing, not as auarice.
\v 6 \f 1 \fr 9.6 \ft The Epiſtle for S. Laurence, Aug. 10.\f* And this I ſay, he that ſovveth ſparingly, ſparingly alſo ſhal reape: and he that ſovveth in bleſſings, of bleſſings alſo ſhal reape.
\v 7 Euery one as he hath determined in his hart, not of ſadnes or of neceſſitie. for \x - \xt Ecclci 35, 11.\x* \it God loueth a cheereful giuer\it*.
\v 8 And God is able to make al grace abound in you: that in al things alvvaies hauing al ſufficiencie, you may abound vnto al good vvorkes,
\v 9 as it is vvritten: \x - \xt Ps. 111, 9.\x* \it He diſtributed, he gaue to the poore: \it* \f 2 \fr 9.9 \ft Ps. 111, 9. · The fruite of almes is the encreaſe of grace in al iuſtice and good workes to life euerlaſting: God giuing theſe things for revvard & recompenſe of charitable workes, which therfore be called the ſeed or meritorious cauſe of theſe ſpiritual fruites.\f* \it his iuſtice\it* remaineth for euer.
\v 10 And he that miniſtreth ſeede to the ſovver, vvil giue bread alſo for to eate: & vvil multiplie your ſeede, and vvil augment the increaſes of the fruites of your iuſtice: ⊢
\v 11 that being enriched in al things, you may abound vnto al ſimplicitie, vvhich worketh by vs thankes-giuing to God.
\v 12 Becauſe the miniſterie of this office doth not only ſupplie thoſe things that the Saincts want, but aboundeth alſo by many thankes-giuings in our Lord,
\v 13 by the proofe of this miniſterie, glorifying God in the obedience of your confeſſion vnto the Goſpel of Chriſt, and in the ſimplicitie of communicating vnto them, and vnto al,
\v 14 and in their praying for you, being deſirous of you becauſe of the excellent grace of God in you.
\v 15 Thankes be to God for his vnſpeakeable gift.
\c 10
\cd Againſt the falſe Apoſtles, graunting the infirmitie of his perſon, he doth notwithſtanding ſet out the power of his Apoſtleſhip, 12 reprehending them alſo for chalenging to them ſelues the praiſe of other mens labours.
\p
\v 1 \sc AND\sc* I Paul my ſelf beſeeche you by the mildenes and modeſtie of Chriſt, vvho in preſence in deede am humble among you, but abſent am bold on you.
\v 2 But I beſeeche you, that being preſent I neede not be bold by that confidence vvhervvith I am thought to be bold againſt ſome: vvhich thinke vs as though vve vvalke according to the fleſh.
\v 3 For vvalking in the fleſh, vve vvarre not according to the fleſh.
\v 4 For the vveapons of our vvarfare are not carnal: but mightie to God vnto the deſtruction of munitions, deſtroying counſels,
\v 5 and al loftineſſe extolling it ſelf againſt the knovvledge of God, and bringing into captiuitie al vnderſtanding vnto the obedience of Chriſt,
\v 6 and hauing in a readineſſe to reuenge al diſobedience, vvhen your obedience ſhal be fulfilled.
\v 7 See the things that are according to appearance. If any man haue affiance in him ſelf, that he is Chriſts: let him thinke this againe vvith him ſelf, that as he is Chriſts, ſo vve alſo.
\v 8 For and if I ſhould glorie ſomevvhat more of our povver, vvhich our Lord hath giuen vs vnto edification and not to your deſtruction: I ſhal not be aſhamed.
\v 9 But that I may not be thought as it vvere to terrifie you by epiſtles (
\v 10 for his epiſtles in deede, ſay they, are ſore and vehement: but his bodily preſence vveake, and his ſpeache contemptible)
\v 11 let him this thinke that is ſuch a one, that ſuch as vve are in vvord by epiſtles, abſent: ſuch alſo we are in deede, preſent.
\v 12 For we dare not matche or compare our ſelues vvith certaine, that commend them ſelues: but vve meaſure our ſelues in our ſelues, and compare our ſelues to our ſelues.
\v 13 But vve vvil not glorie aboue our meaſure: but according to the meaſure of the rule, vvhich God hath meaſured to vs, a meaſure to reache euen vnto you.
\v 14 For not, as though vve reached not vnto you, doe vve extend our ſelues beyond. For we are come as farre as to you in the Goſpel of Chriſt.
\v 15 not glorying aboue meaſure in other mens labours: but hauing \f 1 \fr 10.15 \ft \+it c ἐλπίδα ἀυξομένης τῆς πίςεως ὑμῶν.\+it*\f* hope of your faith increaſing, to be magnified in you according to our rule aboundantly,
\v 16 yea vnto thoſe places that are beyond you, to euangelize, not in an other mans rule, to glorie in thoſe things that are prepared before.
\v 17 \x - \xt Ier. 9, 23.\x* But he that glorieth, let him glorie in our Lord. \f 2 \fr 10.17 \ft The Epiſtle upon S. Lucy's day 13. Decem. S. Martha's. Iulij 29. S. Agnes. ſecundo Ian. 28. S. Potentiana's. May 19.\f*
\v 18 For not he that commendeth him ſelf, the ſame is approued: but vvhom God commendeth.
\c 11
\cd He reaſoneth the matter vvith the Corinthians, why they ſhould preferre the falſe Apoſtles before him. 16 And becauſe they giue them leaue to bragge and commend them ſelues, and to abuſe them ſo miſerably, he truſteth they vvil alſo giue him the hearing: 21 and ſo he beginneth, and firſt ſhewing him ſelf in al Iudaical reſpectes (wherein onely ſtood al their boaſting) to be as they are, he addeth afterward ſuch a long roll of his ſufferings for Chriſt, as is incomparable.
\p
\v 1 VVould God you could beare ſome litle of my folly: but do ye alſo ſupport me:
\v 2 for I emulate you vvith the emulation of God. For I haue \f 1 \fr 11.2 \ft The Apoſtles and their ſucceſſors did deſpouſe the people whom they conuerted, to Chriſt, in al puritie & Chaſtitie of truth, and wholy vndefiled and void of errour and hereſie.\f* deſpouſed you to one man, to preſent you a chaſte virgin vnto Chriſt.
\v 3 \x - \xt Gen. 3, 4.\x* But I feare leſt, as the ſerpent ſeduced Eue by his ſubteltie, ſo your ſenſes may be corrupted, & fall from the ſimplicitie that is in Chriſt.
\v 4 For if he that \f 1 \fr 11.4 \ft The note of a falſe teacher, \+it to come\+it*: that is, without lawful calling or ſending to thruſt and intrude him ſelf into an other mans charge.\f* commeth, preache an other Chriſt whom we haue not preached, or you receiue an other ſpirit vvhom you haue not receiued: or an other Goſpel vvhich you haue not receiued: you might wel ſuffer it.
\v 5 For I ſuppoſe that I haue done nothing leſſe then the great Apoſtles.
\v 6 For although rude in ſpeache, yet not in knovvledge. but in al things we are made manifeſt to you.
\v 7 Or did I commit a ſinne, humbling my ſelf, that you might be exalted? becauſe I euangelized vnto you the Goſpel of God gratis?
\v 8 Other churches I ſpoiled, taking a ſtipend, for your miniſterie.
\v 9 And when I vvas vvith you, and had neede, I vvas burdenous to none: for that which I vvanted, the brethren ſupplied that came from Macedonia: & in al things I haue kept my ſelf vvithout burden to you, and vvil keepe.
\v 10 The truth of Chriſt is in me, that this glorying ſhal not be infringed tovvard me in the countries of Achaia.
\v 11 Wherfore? becauſe I loue you not? God doth knovv.
\v 12 But that vvhich I doe, I vvil alſo doe, that I may cut avvay the occaſion of them that deſire occaſion: that, in that vvhich they glorie, they may be found euen like vs.
\v 13 For ſuch falſe apoſtles are \f 1 \fr 11.13 \ft A proper terme for Heretikes that ſhape them ſelues into the habit of true teachers, ſpecially by often allegation and commendation of the Scriptures. Reade the notable admonition of the auncient writer \+it Vincentius Lirinenſis\+it* in his golden booke \+it Againſt the Prophane nouelties of al hereſies\+it*.\f* craftie vvorkers, trāsfiguring them ſelues into Apoſtles of Chriſt.
\v 14 And no maruel: for Satan him ſelf transfigureth him ſelf into an Angel of light.
\v 15 It is no great matter therfore if his miniſters be transfigured as the miniſters of iuſtice: vvhoſe ende ſhal be according to their vvorkes.
\v 16 Againe I ſay, (let no man thinke me to be fooliſh: otherwiſe take me as fooliſh, that I alſo may glorie a litle,)
\v 17 that vvhich I ſpeake, I ſpeake not according to God, but as it vvere in fooliſhnes, in this ſubſtance of glorying.
\v 18 Becauſe many glorie according to the fleſh, I alſo wil glorie.
\v 19 \f 1 \fr 11.19 \ft The Epiſtle vpon the Sunday of Sexageſime.\f* For you do gladly ſuffer the fooliſh: vvhereas your ſelues are wiſe.
\v 20 For you ſuffer if a man bring you into ſeruitude, if a man deuoure, if a man take, if a man be extolled, if a man ſtrike you on the face.
\v 21 I ſpeake according to diſhonour, as though vve had been vveake in this part. VVherein any man dare (I ſpeake foliſhly) I dare alſo.
\v 22 \x - \xt Phil. 3, 5.\x* They are Hebrevves: and I. They are Iſraëlites: and I. They are the ſeede of Abraham: and I.
\v 23 They are the miniſters of Chriſt: and I. (I ſpeake as one ſcarſe vviſe) more I: in many moe labours, in priſons more aboundantly, in ſtripes aboue meaſure, in deathes often.
\v 24 Of the Ievves fiue times, did I receiue \x - \xt Deu. 25, 3.\x* fourtie, ſauing one.
\v 25 Thriſe vvas I beaten \x - \xt Act. 16, 23.\x* vvith roddes, \x - \xt 14, 18.\x* once I vvas ſtoned, thriſe I ſuffred \x - \xt 27, 15.\x* ſhipvvracke, night and day haue I been in the depth of the ſea,
\v 26 in iourneying often, perils of vvaters, perils of theeues, perils of my nation, perils of Gentiles, perils in the citie, perils in the wildernes, perils in the ſea, perils among falſe brethren,
\v 27 in labour and miſerie, in much vvatchings, in hunger and thirſt, in faſtings often, in colde and nakednes,
\v 28 beſide thoſe things which are outwardly: my daily \f 1 \fr 11.28 \ft \+it ἐπισύστασις\+it*. S. Chryſoſtom and Theophylacte interpret it of daily conſpiracie againſt him. others, of multitude of cares inſtant & vrgent vpon him.\f* binſtance, the carefulnes of al churches.
\v 29 VVho is vveake, and I \f 1 \fr 11.29 \ft \+it non vror? πυροῦμαι\+it*?\f* am not vveake? vvho is ſcandalized, and I cam not burnt?
\v 30 If I muſt glorie: I vvil glorie of the things that concerne my infirmitie.
\v 31 The God and Father of our Lord \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt, vvho is bleſſed for euer, knovveth that I lie not.
\v 32 \x - \xt Act. 9, 24.\x* At Damaſcus the Gouernour of the nation vnder Aretas the king, kept the citie of the Damaſcenes, for to apprehend me:
\v 33 and through a vvindovv in a baſket vvas I let dovvne by the vvall, and ſo eſcaped his handes.
\c 12
\cd He telleth of his incomparable viſions, 5 but for humilitie liketh better to talke of his infirmities: 11 putting the fault in the Corinthians for that he is faine thus to rehearſe his owne commendations. 13 Where againe he reaſoneth the matter with them like a father, why they ſhould preferre thoſe falſe Apoſtles before him. 20 And feareth leſt at his comming he ſhal be compelled to excommunicate many of them.
\p
\v 1 IF I muſt glorie (it is not expedient in deede) but I vvil come to the viſions and reuelations of our Lord.
\v 2 I knovv a man in Chriſt aboue fourtene yeres agoe (vvhether in the body, I knovv not: or out of the body, I knovv not: God doth knovv) ſuch a one \f 1 \fr 12.2 \ft By this we may proue that it is neither impoſſible, incredible, nor vndecent, that is reported by the auncient fathers of ſome that haue been rauiſhed or rapt (vvhether in body or out of body God knoweth) & brought to ſee the ſtate of the next life, as wel of the ſaued as damned.\f* rapt euen to the third heauen.
\v 3 And I know ſuch a man (vvhether in the body, or out of the body, I know not: God doth know)
\v 4 that he vvas rapt into Paradiſe: & heard ſecrete vvordes, which it is not lavvful for a man to ſpeake.
\v 5 For ſuch an one I wil glorie: but for my ſelf I vvil glorie nothing, ſauing in my infirmities.
\v 6 For and if I vvil glorie, I ſhal not be fooliſh: for I ſhal ſay truth. but I ſpare, leſt any man ſhould eſteeme me aboue that vvhich he ſeeth in me, or heareth any thing of me.
\v 7 And leſt the greatnes of the reuelations might extoll me, there vvas giuen me a pricke of my fleſh, an angel of Satan, to buffet me.
\v 8 For the vvhich thing thriſe I beſought our Lord, that it might depart from me:
\v 9 and he ſaid to me, My grace ſufficeth thee, for povver is perfited in infirmitie. Gladly therfore vvil I glorie in mine infirmitie, that the povver of Chriſt may dvvel in me. ⊢
\v 10 For the vvhich cauſe I pleaſe my ſelf in infirmities, in contumelies, in neceſſities, in perſecutions, in diſtreſſes for Chriſt. for vvhen I am vveake, then am I mightie.
\v 11 I am become fooliſh: you haue compelled me. For I ought to haue been commended of you: for I haue been nothing leſſe then they that are aboue meaſure Apoſtles: although I am nothing.
\v 12 Yet the ſignes of my Apoſtleſhip haue been done vpon you in al patience, in ſignes & wonders and mighty deedes.
\v 13 For vvhat is there that you haue had leſſe then the other churches: but that I my ſelf haue not burdened you? Pardon me this iniurie.
\v 14 Behold, novv the third time I am ready to come to you: and I vvil not be burdenous vnto you. For I ſeeke not the things that are yours: but you. For neither ought the children lay vp treaſures for the parents, but the parents for the children.
\v 15 But I moſt gladly vvil beſtovv, & vvil my ſelf moreouer be beſtovved for your ſoules: although louing you more, I am loued leſſe.
\v 16 But be it ſo: I haue not burdened you: but being craftie, I tooke you by guile.
\v 17 Haue I circumuented you by any of them vvhom I ſent to you?
\v 18 I requeſted Titus, and I ſent vvith him a brother. Did Titus circumuent you? walked vve not vvith one ſpirit? not in the ſelf ſame ſteppes?
\v 19 \f 1 \fr 12.19 \fq Of old \ft \+it Againe\+it*.\f* Of old thinke you that vve excuſe our ſelues to you? Before God, in Chriſt vve ſpeake: but al things (my deereſt) for your edifying.
\v 20 For I feare leſt perhaps vvhen I come, I finde you not ſuch as I vvould: and I be found of you, ſuch an one as you vvould not. leſt perhaps contentions, emulations, ſtomakings, diſſenſions, detractions, vvhiſperings, ſwellings, ſeditions be among you.
\v 21 leſt againe vvhen I come, God humble me among you: & I mourne many of them that ſinned before, & \f 1 \fr 12.21 \ft \+it τῶν μὴ μετανοησάντων\+it*. Which S. Auguſtine ſaith \+it (ep. 108\+it*) is ſpoken here of doing great penance for heinous ſinnes, as \+it Pœnitentes\+it* did in the primitiue Church. So that it is not onely to repent or to amend their liues, as the Proteſtants tranſlate it.\f* haue not done penance for the vncleannes & fornication and incontinencie that they haue committed.
\c 13
\cd He driueth into them the feare of excommunication: to the end that they doing penance beforehand, he may not be compelled to vſe his authoritie when he commeth, and as he hath threatened. 11 And ſo with a general exhortation he endeth.
\p
\v 1 LO this the third time I come vnto you: \x - \xt Deu. 19, 15.\x* In the mouth of tvvo or three vvitneſſes ſhal euery vvord ſtand.
\v 2 I foretold and doe foretel as preſent, and novv abſent, to them that ſinned before, and al the reſt, that if I come againe, I vvil not ſpare.
\v 3 Seeke you an experiment of him that ſpeaketh in me, Chriſt: vvho in you is not vveake, but is mightie in you?
\v 4 For although he vvas crucified of infirmitie: yet he liueth by the povver of God. For vve alſo are vveake in him: but vve ſhal liue vvith him by the povver of God on you.
\v 5 Trie your ovvne ſelues if you be in the faith: proue ye your ſelues. Knovv you not your ſelues that Chriſt \sc IESVS\sc* is in you, vnleſſe perhaps you be reprobates.
\v 6 But I hope you \f 1 \fr 13.6 \fq bknow \ft ſhal know.\f* bknow that vve are not reprobates.
\v 7 And vve pray God that you doe no euil, not that vve may appeare approued, but that you may doe that vvhich is good, and vve be as reprobates.
\v 8 For we can not any thing againſt the truth: but for the truth.
\v 9 For vve reioyce, for that vve are vveake, & you are mightie. This alſo vve pray for, your conſummation.
\v 10 Therfore theſe things I vvrite abſent: that being preſent I may not deale hardly according to the \f 1 \fr 13.10 \ft Eccleſiaſtical power to puniſh offenders by the cēſures of the Church.\f* povver vvhich our Lord hath giuen me vnto edification and not vnto deſtruction.
\v 11 \x - \xt c παρακαλεῖσθε, Ro. 16, 16. 1. Cor. 16, 20.\x* For the reſt brethren, reioyce, be perfect, \f 2 \fr 13.11 \ft The Epiſtle in a votiue Maſſe of the B. Trinitie.\f* take exhortation, be of one minde, haue peace, and the God of peace & of loue ſhal be vvith you.
\v 12 Salute one an other in a holy kiſſe. Al the ſaincts ſalute you.
\v 13 The grace of our Lord \sc IESVS\sc* Chriſt, and the charitie of God, and the communication of the holy Ghoſt be vvith you all. Amen.
